## Final Judgement
## Step 1: Summary of Key Insights

The fact-check reveals that the claim contains a partial truth and a clear factual error. Regarding the popular vote, Donald Trump did lose the 2016 popular vote by approximately 2,864,974 votes (roughly 3 million), which supports the first part of the claim. However, regarding Supreme Court nominations, as of October 13, 2020, Trump had nominated only two justices—Neil Gorsuch and Brett Kavanaugh—out of a total of nine sitting justices. Two out of nine is approximately 22.2%, which falls well short of "a full third" (33.3%) as claimed. The evidence is clear on both points, and no further investigation is required.

## Step 2: Missing Information

No additional information is needed; the evidence is sufficient to render a decision on both parts of the claim.

## Step 3: Conclusion

The claim asserts that Donald Trump nominated "a full third" of the Supreme Court as of October 13, 2020. The factual record shows he had nominated two out of nine justices, which is roughly two-ninths—not one-third. While the claim about losing the popular vote by approximately 3 million is supported, the central and more substantive assertion about the fraction of the Court is factually incorrect. Therefore, the claim as a whole is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is **partially true but ultimately refuted** because while Donald Trump did lose the 2016 popular vote by nearly 2.86 million votes, as [certified results confirm](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/hillary-clinton-officially-wins-popular-vote-29-million/story?id=44354341), he had nominated only two of the nine sitting Supreme Court justices (Neil Gorsuch and Brett Kavanaugh) as of October 13, 2020, which is approximately 22% and [less than one-third](https://www.scotusblog.com/2020/09/trump-releases-new-list-of-potential-supreme-court-nominees/), not "a full third" as claimed.